    {
      "id": 17,
      "type": "MarkdownNote",
      "pos": [
        0,
        -560
      ],
      "size": [
        1000,
        440
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 2,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "## A Siamese network, and what weight sharing is for\n\nThis model never learns the classes. It learns a **comparison** — two things go in and one\nbit comes out: *are these the same?*\n\nThat is why it can be asked about classes it has never seen, and it is the shape behind\nface verification, signature checking and one-shot learning.\n\nThe trick is that both towers are **one set of weights, used twice**. Forking a wire\nduplicates data, not weights, so the two MLP Block nodes carry the same `share` tag,\n`tower`. Give them different tags and you get two independent towers that see the world\ndifferently, and the comparison stops meaning anything. The summary says\n`MLP Block (shared)` on the second one."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
    {
      "id": 18,
      "type": "MarkdownNote",
      "pos": [
        1030,
        -560
      ],
      "size": [
        640,
        440
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 3,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "### Two inputs, not one\n\n**Dataset As Pairs** draws pairs from a labelled dataset — half from the same class, half\nfrom different ones, so 50% is chance and the accuracy means something.\n\nIt hands them over as **two separate inputs**, which is why there are two Input nodes here\nand why both take their shape from the same socket.\n\nEverywhere else in this pack a model takes one thing. This is the case that needs two, and\nthe Train node feeds them in the order the Input nodes were traced: `left`, then\n`right`."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
    {
      "id": 19,
      "type": "MarkdownNote",
      "pos": [
        1380,
        560
      ],
      "size": [
        640,
        320
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 4,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "### Comparing without being told how\n\n**Subtract**, then **Multiply by itself**: the squared difference along each of the 16\ndimensions the tower learned.\n\nThe Linear on the end turns those 16 numbers into \"same\" or \"different\", so the network\ndecides which parts of the comparison matter instead of being handed a distance formula."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    }
